what is react portals?
React Portals allow a component to render its DOM nodes outside its parent DOM hierarchy while remaining part of the same React component tree. 
They are created using createPortal(). Portals are commonly used for modals, tooltips, and dropdowns where rendering inside the parent container may cause overflow or z-index issues. 
Even though the DOM is rendered elsewhere, React event bubbling still works through the React component tree.


Portal setup
1)In public/index.html:

<body>
  <div id="root"></div>
  <div id="portal-root"></div>
</body>


2)
import { useState } from "react";
import { createPortal } from "react-dom";

function Modal({ onClose }) {
  return createPortal(
    <div className="overlay">
      <div className="modal">
        <h2>Delete Item?</h2>

        <button onClick={onClose}>
          Close
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>,
    document.body
  );
}

export default function App() {
  const [showModal, setShowModal] = useState(false);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setShowModal(true)}>
        Open Modal
      </button>

      {showModal && (
        <Modal
          onClose={() => setShowModal(false)}
        />
      )}
    </>
  );
}

Does event bubbling and event capturing occurs?
Event capturing and bubbling both work through the React tree even when a component is rendered using a Portal.
Yes. With React Portals, both event capturing (onClickCapture) and event bubbling (onClick) work through the React component tree, not the DOM tree.
Even if a portal renders its DOM nodes elsewhere, React's synthetic event system preserves the parent-child relationship from the React hierarchy, so capture and bubble phases continue to work as expected.
